 "arms": {
  "full": {
   "text": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was a prominent figure in the 11th century and played a key role in the political and military affairs of the County of Tripoli. He was the son of Raymond II and was born in 1020. Raymond III was a strong advocate for the interests of the Crusader states and was involved in the broader context of the Crusades. His reign was marked by efforts to maintain the independence of the County of Tripoli against the growing influence of the Byzantine Empire and the Muslim powers in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was a key figure in the Crusader presence in the region and supported the Crusader cause. His leadership helped to establish and strengthen the Crusader presence in Lebanon, a crucial area for the Crusader states. The County of Tripoli was strategically located in Lebanon, and Raymond III's efforts were vital in securing the region's position.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nDuring his reign, Raymond III faced significant challenges from the Muslim powers, particularly the Seljuk Empire. He was involved in several conflicts with the Muslim forces and worked to defend the County against invasions. His military campaigns were a crucial part of the broader struggle between the Crusader states and the Muslim world.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III's rule ended in 1055 when he was defeated by the Byzantine Emperor Alexios Komnenos. His defeat marked the end of the Count of Tripoli's influence in the region and led to the eventual loss of the County to the Byzantine Empire. The loss of Tripoli to the Byzantines had significant implications for the Crusader presence in Lebanon and the broader region.\n\nthis overview provides a glimpse into the complexities and intrigues of crusader-era politics and warfare.",
